// ─── Module 0: Mechanics ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.science.m0 = {
  0: {emoji:"🚐", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Why things move — and why they don't",
    illusText:"Every time a taxi brakes and you lurch forward, Newton's first law is happening to you.",
    hook:{title:"Why you fly forward when the taxi brakes", body:"The taxi driver slams on the brakes at a robot. The taxi stops — but your body keeps moving forward until the seatbelt (or the seat in front) stops you. Nothing pushed you forward: your body simply <strong>kept doing what it was doing</strong>. That's inertia, and it's why seatbelts save thousands of lives on South African roads every year."},
    intro:"Newton's three laws describe how forces change motion. <strong>First law (inertia):</strong> an object stays at rest or moves at constant velocity unless a resultant (net) force acts on it. <strong>Second law:</strong> F_net = ma — the resultant force equals mass times acceleration, in the direction of the force. <strong>Third law:</strong> when A exerts a force on B, B exerts an equal and opposite force on A.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🛑 First law — inertia", body:"Mass is a measure of inertia: a loaded truck is harder to start and stop than a bicycle. Seatbelts, headrests and airbags exist because your body obeys the first law in a crash."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⚖️ Second law — F_net = ma", body:"Add all forces (with directions) to get F_net, then a = F_net/m. Double the force → double the acceleration. Double the mass → half the acceleration. Units: 1 N = 1 kg·m·s⁻²."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔁 Third law — action-reaction pairs", body:"The pair acts on <strong>different</strong> objects, so they never cancel each other. A swimmer pushes water backwards; the water pushes the swimmer forwards. A rocket pushes gas down; the gas pushes the rocket up."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Accelerating taxi", body:"A 1 500 kg taxi accelerates at 2 m·s⁻². What resultant force acts on it?", answer:"F_net = ma = 1 500 × 2 = <strong>3 000 N</strong> forward."},
      {title:"Two forces", body:"A 20 kg box is pulled with 100 N to the right while friction is 60 N to the left. Find a.", answer:"F_net = 100 − 60 = 40 N right. a = 40/20 = <strong>2 m·s⁻² to the right</strong>."},
      {title:"Third-law pair", body:"Name the reaction to: “the Earth pulls a soccer ball down”.", answer:"<strong>The ball pulls the Earth up</strong> with an equal force. (The normal force from the ground is NOT the reaction — it acts on the same object.)"},
    ],
    formula:"F_net = ma",
    formulaNote:"Always state the direction of force and acceleration — they are vectors.",
    mistakes:["Using the applied force instead of the resultant force in F = ma.","Saying a moving object needs a force to keep moving at constant velocity (it doesn't).","Thinking third-law forces cancel — they act on different objects."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Vehicle safety design, sports science (why a Springbok prop is hard to stop), aerospace and civil engineering all start from Newton's laws."},
    activity:"A 60 kg learner in a lift accelerates upward at 1.5 m·s⁻². What normal force does the floor exert? (Use g = 9.8 m·s⁻².)",
    activityAnswer:"F_net = N − mg = ma → N = m(g + a) = 60 × 11.3 = 678 N upward. (They feel heavier.)",
    examTip:"Draw a free-body diagram first, choose a positive direction, then write F_net = ma. The NSC gives marks for each of these steps.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Inertia",d:"Property of an object to resist a change in its state of motion"},{w:"Resultant force",d:"Single force with the same effect as all forces combined"},{w:"Newton (N)",d:"Force that gives 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m·s⁻²"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A 2 kg object has a resultant force of 10 N. Its acceleration is:",opts:["20 m·s⁻²","5 m·s⁻²","0.2 m·s⁻²","12 m·s⁻²"],a:1,et:"a = F/m = 10/2 = 5 m·s⁻²."},
      {q:"An object moving at constant velocity has a resultant force of:",opts:["Zero","Equal to its weight","In the direction of motion","Increasing"],a:0,et:"Constant velocity means no acceleration, so F_net = 0 (first law)."},
      {q:"Action-reaction forces:",opts:["Act on the same object","Cancel out","Act on different objects","Have different sizes"],a:2,et:"Newton's third law pairs act on different objects, so they don't cancel."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Three laws explain every push and pull you'll ever feel. Draw the forces, find the resultant, and F = ma does the rest."},

  1: {emoji:"📦", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Drawing every force on an object",
    illusText:"Before you calculate anything, draw the forces. A good free-body diagram solves half the problem.",
    hook:{title:"Loading the bakkie", body:"A farmer in the Free State slides a 10 kg feed bag up a plank into his bakkie. Gravity pulls it straight down, the plank pushes back perpendicular to its surface, friction drags against the motion and the farmer pushes it up the slope. Four forces, four directions. A <strong>free-body diagram</strong> sorts them out so you can work out exactly how hard he must push."},
    intro:"A <strong>free-body diagram</strong> shows one object as a dot or box with every force acting on it drawn as an arrow from the object. Common forces: <strong>weight</strong> w = mg (down), <strong>normal force</strong> N (perpendicular to the surface), <strong>friction</strong> f (parallel to the surface, opposing motion), <strong>applied force</strong>, and <strong>tension</strong> in ropes. Friction: f_s(max) = μ_s N (static) and f_k = μ_k N (kinetic).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"✏️ Drawing rules", body:"One object only. Arrows start at the object and point away. Label each arrow (w, N, f, F_A, T). Lengths should roughly show relative size. Don't draw forces the object exerts on other things."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⛰️ Slopes", body:"On a slope at angle θ, split weight into components: mg sin θ down the slope and mg cos θ into the slope. Then N = mg cos θ (if nothing else pushes into the slope)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧲 Friction", body:"Static friction stops something starting to slide (up to μ_s N). Once moving, kinetic friction f_k = μ_k N acts. μ has no units; rough surfaces have bigger μ."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Feed bag on a slope", body:"A 10 kg bag rests on a 30° plank. Find the component of its weight down the slope.", answer:"mg sin θ = 10 × 9.8 × sin 30° = <strong>49 N down the slope</strong>."},
      {title:"Normal force on the slope", body:"Same bag.", answer:"N = mg cos θ = 10 × 9.8 × cos 30° ≈ <strong>84.9 N</strong> perpendicular to the plank."},
      {title:"Kinetic friction", body:"μ_k = 0.3 between the bag and plank. Friction while sliding?", answer:"f_k = μ_k N = 0.3 × 84.9 ≈ <strong>25.5 N</strong>, opposing the motion."},
    ],
    formula:"w = mg · f_k = μ_k N · on a slope: mg sin θ (along), mg cos θ (perpendicular)",
    mistakes:["Drawing a “force of motion” — there is no such force.","Making N = mg on a slope (it's mg cos θ).","Including forces that act on other objects."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Engineers use free-body diagrams to design bridges, cranes, ramps and car brakes. Physiotherapists use them to analyse forces on joints."},
    activity:"Draw the free-body diagram of a picture hanging from two strings, then list each force.",
    activityAnswer:"Weight (down), tension in string 1 (along string 1, away from the picture), tension in string 2 (along string 2). Three forces; at rest they add to zero.",
    examTip:"Free-body diagrams are often worth 3–4 marks on their own. Use arrows with labels, and don't add extra forces.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Free-body diagram",d:"Diagram of one object with all forces acting on it"},{w:"Normal force",d:"Perpendicular force exerted by a surface"},{w:"Coefficient of friction (μ)",d:"Ratio of friction to normal force; no units"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The normal force always acts:",opts:["Downwards","Perpendicular to the surface","Along the surface","Opposite to motion"],a:1,et:"Normal means perpendicular to the contact surface."},
      {q:"Weight of a 5 kg object (g = 9.8 m·s⁻²):",opts:["5 N","49 N","9.8 N","0.5 N"],a:1,et:"w = mg = 5 × 9.8 = 49 N."},
      {q:"If μ_k = 0.2 and N = 100 N, kinetic friction is:",opts:["20 N","500 N","0.002 N","100 N"],a:0,et:"f_k = μ_k N = 0.2 × 100 = 20 N."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Draw the diagram, resolve the forces, then apply Newton's second law. Every mechanics question gets easier this way."},

  2: {emoji:"🏉", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Mass in motion and how collisions work",
    illusText:"A 120 kg prop running at full speed is hard to stop. Momentum measures exactly how hard.",
    hook:{title:"Why airbags save lives", body:"In a crash, your car's momentum must drop to zero. The force on you depends on <strong>how quickly</strong> that happens. An airbag stretches the stopping time from a few milliseconds to much longer, so the force on your head is many times smaller. Same change in momentum, smaller force — that's impulse, and it's built into every modern car."},
    intro:"<strong>Momentum</strong> p = mv (kg·m·s⁻¹) is a vector in the direction of velocity. Newton's second law in momentum form: F_net = Δp/Δt. <strong>Impulse</strong> = F_net Δt = Δp. In an isolated system (no external resultant force), <strong>momentum is conserved</strong>: total momentum before = total momentum after.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⏱️ Impulse", body:"For the same Δp, a longer Δt means a smaller force. That's why you bend your knees when landing, why cricket fielders pull their hands back and why crumple zones exist."},
      {t:"teal", title:"💥 Conservation of momentum", body:"Σp_before = Σp_after. Choose a positive direction and give velocities in the other direction a negative sign."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🎱 Elastic vs inelastic", body:"Elastic: kinetic energy is also conserved (rare — e.g. snooker balls approximately). Inelastic: some kinetic energy becomes heat, sound and deformation (most real crashes)."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"A prop's momentum", body:"A 100 kg prop runs at 6 m·s⁻². Find his momentum.", answer:"p = mv = 100 × 6 = <strong>600 kg·m·s⁻¹</strong> in his running direction."},
      {title:"Bakkie hits a parked car", body:"A 2 000 kg bakkie at 10 m·s⁻¹ hits a stationary 1 000 kg car and they move off together.", answer:"2 000 × 10 = 3 000 × v → v ≈ <strong>6.67 m·s⁻¹</strong> in the bakkie's direction."},
      {title:"Kicking a ball", body:"A 0.45 kg ball arrives at 10 m·s⁻¹ and is kicked back at 20 m·s⁻¹. Contact lasts 0.01 s. Find the average force.", answer:"Take the kick direction positive: Δp = 0.45(20 − (−10)) = 13.5 kg·m·s⁻¹. F = Δp/Δt = <strong>1 350 N</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"p = mv · F_net Δt = Δp · Σp_before = Σp_after",
    mistakes:["Forgetting the negative sign for a velocity in the opposite direction.","Saying momentum is conserved when an external force (like friction) acts significantly.","Mixing up impulse (N·s) with force (N)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Road-accident reconstruction by the SAPS, car safety engineering, rocket science and sports coaching all use momentum and impulse."},
    activity:"A 0.05 kg bullet leaves a 4 kg rifle at 400 m·s⁻¹. Find the rifle's recoil velocity.",
    activityAnswer:"0 = 0.05 × 400 + 4v → v = −5 m·s⁻¹, i.e. 5 m·s⁻¹ backwards.",
    examTip:"Write “Σp_before = Σp_after” first, define your positive direction, then substitute. Answers must include direction.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Momentum",d:"Product of mass and velocity; vector"},{w:"Impulse",d:"F_net × Δt; equals change in momentum"},{w:"Isolated system",d:"No external resultant force acts on it"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Momentum of a 3 kg ball at 4 m·s⁻¹:",opts:["7 kg·m·s⁻¹","12 kg·m·s⁻¹","0.75 kg·m·s⁻¹","48 kg·m·s⁻¹"],a:1,et:"p = mv = 3 × 4 = 12 kg·m·s⁻¹."},
      {q:"Airbags reduce injury because they:",opts:["Reduce Δp","Increase Δt","Increase mass","Remove momentum"],a:1,et:"Same Δp over a longer Δt means a smaller force."},
      {q:"In an inelastic collision:",opts:["Momentum is lost","Kinetic energy is conserved","Momentum is conserved but kinetic energy is not","Nothing is conserved"],a:2,et:"Momentum is conserved in an isolated system; some kinetic energy turns into other forms."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Momentum explains collisions, and impulse explains why soft landings hurt less. Always keep track of direction."},

  3: {emoji:"🏗️", subtitle:"Grade 12 · How energy is transferred by forces",
    illusText:"Lifting bricks up a scaffold takes work. Doing it quickly takes power.",
    hook:{title:"How powerful must the hoist be?", body:"A construction company in Durban lifts 600 kg of bricks 30 m up a building in 20 seconds. The work done against gravity is mgh = 176 400 J, so the motor must deliver at least <strong>8 820 W</strong> (about 8.8 kW). Engineers size every motor, pump and lift with exactly this calculation."},
    intro:"<strong>Work</strong> is done when a force moves an object: W = FΔx cos θ, where θ is the angle between the force and the displacement (unit: joule, J). <strong>Kinetic energy</strong> E_k = ½mv². The <strong>work-energy theorem</strong>: the net work done on an object equals its change in kinetic energy, W_net = ΔE_k. <strong>Power</strong> is the rate of doing work: P = W/Δt (watt, W), and for constant velocity P_ave = Fv_ave.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"↗️ The angle matters", body:"Pushing a trolley at 30° below the horizontal: only F cos 30° does useful work. A force perpendicular to the motion (like the normal force on a flat road) does zero work."},
      {t:"teal", title:"➕ Positive and negative work", body:"Force in the direction of motion → positive work (adds energy). Friction opposing motion → negative work (removes energy, usually as heat)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"⚡ Power", body:"Two learners climb the same stairs; the faster one has more power, even though they do the same work. 1 W = 1 J·s⁻¹."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Pulling a trolley", body:"A shopper pulls a trolley 5 m with 200 N at 30° above the horizontal.", answer:"W = FΔx cos θ = 200 × 5 × cos 30° ≈ <strong>866 J</strong>."},
      {title:"The brick hoist", body:"600 kg raised 30 m in 20 s.", answer:"W = mgh = 600 × 9.8 × 30 = 176 400 J. P = W/t = <strong>8 820 W</strong>."},
      {title:"Work-energy theorem", body:"A 1 000 kg car speeds up from 10 to 20 m·s⁻¹. Find W_net.", answer:"ΔE_k = ½ × 1 000 × (20² − 10²) = <strong>150 000 J</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"W = FΔx cos θ · E_k = ½mv² · W_net = ΔE_k · P = W/Δt",
    mistakes:["Using sin θ instead of cos θ for the angle between force and displacement.","Forgetting to square v in E_k.","Using (20 − 10)² instead of 20² − 10² in ΔE_k."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Mechanical and electrical engineering, choosing a generator for load shedding (power ratings), sports science and lift design."},
    activity:"A 50 kg learner climbs 4 m of stairs in 5 s. What is their average power output?",
    activityAnswer:"W = mgh = 50 × 9.8 × 4 = 1 960 J. P = 1 960/5 = 392 W.",
    examTip:"State the work-energy theorem in words when asked to “use the work-energy theorem” — the definition itself is a mark.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Work",d:"Energy transferred by a force: FΔx cos θ"},{w:"Kinetic energy",d:"Energy of motion: ½mv²"},{w:"Power",d:"Rate of doing work, in watts"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A force of 50 N moves a box 10 m in the direction of the force. Work done:",opts:["5 J","60 J","500 J","0 J"],a:2,et:"W = 50 × 10 × cos 0° = 500 J."},
      {q:"The normal force on a car on a flat road does:",opts:["Positive work","Negative work","Zero work","Maximum work"],a:2,et:"It's perpendicular to the motion: cos 90° = 0."},
      {q:"1 200 J of work in 4 s is a power of:",opts:["300 W","4 800 W","1 196 W","0.003 W"],a:0,et:"P = W/t = 1 200/4 = 300 W."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Work moves energy, power measures how fast. These two ideas size every engine, motor and athlete."},

  4: {emoji:"🎢", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Energy changes form but never disappears",
    illusText:"At the top of a roller coaster you have potential energy. At the bottom it has become speed.",
    hook:{title:"The roller coaster drop at Gold Reef City", body:"A roller coaster car is pulled to the top of a 40 m drop and released. Ignoring friction, all its gravitational potential energy turns into kinetic energy: at the bottom it moves at <strong>28 m·s⁻¹ — about 100 km/h</strong>. Ride designers use this exact calculation (plus friction) to make rides thrilling but safe."},
    intro:"<strong>Gravitational potential energy</strong> E_p = mgh. <strong>Kinetic energy</strong> E_k = ½mv². <strong>Mechanical energy</strong> E_M = E_p + E_k. When only gravity does work (no friction or air resistance), mechanical energy is <strong>conserved</strong>: E_M at the top = E_M at the bottom.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔄 Energy swaps", body:"As an object falls, E_p decreases and E_k increases by the same amount. At the highest point of a swing, E_k is smallest; at the lowest point, E_k is greatest."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🚫 Mass cancels", body:"mgh = ½mv² → v = √(2gh). The speed at the bottom doesn't depend on mass (without friction)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔥 With friction", body:"Friction turns some mechanical energy into heat and sound, so E_M at the bottom is less. Total energy is still conserved — it just isn't all mechanical."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"The 40 m drop", body:"Speed at the bottom, no friction.", answer:"v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 40) = √784 = <strong>28 m·s⁻¹</strong> (≈ 100 km/h)."},
      {title:"Mechanical energy", body:"A 2 kg ball is 5 m above the ground moving at 3 m·s⁻¹.", answer:"E_p = 2 × 9.8 × 5 = 98 J; E_k = ½ × 2 × 9 = 9 J; <strong>E_M = 107 J</strong>."},
      {title:"Pendulum", body:"A pendulum bob is released 0.2 m above its lowest point. Speed at the bottom?", answer:"v = √(2 × 9.8 × 0.2) ≈ <strong>1.98 m·s⁻¹</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"E_p = mgh · E_k = ½mv² · (E_p + E_k)_top = (E_p + E_k)_bottom",
    mistakes:["Using conservation of mechanical energy when friction is present.","Measuring h from the wrong reference point.","Forgetting the square root when solving for v."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Hydro-electric schemes like Drakensberg pumped storage turn water's potential energy into electricity. Ride design, bungee jumping (Bloukrans) and sport all use energy conservation."},
    activity:"A 0.5 kg ball is thrown up at 8 m·s⁻¹. Use energy conservation to find the maximum height.",
    activityAnswer:"½mv² = mgh → h = v²/2g = 64/19.6 ≈ 3.27 m.",
    examTip:"State the principle in words: “In an isolated system the total mechanical energy is conserved.” It's often a 2-mark definition.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Mechanical energy",d:"Sum of gravitational potential and kinetic energy"},{w:"Isolated system",d:"No external forces like friction do work"},{w:"Joule (J)",d:"SI unit of energy and work"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"E_p of a 3 kg object 10 m high:",opts:["30 J","294 J","300 J","29.4 J"],a:1,et:"E_p = mgh = 3 × 9.8 × 10 = 294 J."},
      {q:"At the lowest point of a swing, E_k is:",opts:["Zero","Maximum","Minimum","Equal to E_p"],a:1,et:"Most potential energy has turned into kinetic energy."},
      {q:"Without friction, a heavier ball dropped from the same height lands:",opts:["Faster","Slower","At the same speed","With zero energy"],a:2,et:"v = √(2gh) doesn't depend on mass."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Energy changes form but the total stays the same. Find the energy at the start, set it equal to the end, and solve."},

  5: {emoji:"🏏", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Up, down and free fall",
    illusText:"Toss a cricket ball straight up: it slows, stops for an instant, then falls back — all with the same acceleration.",
    hook:{title:"How high can you throw a cricket ball?", body:"A fielder throws a ball straight up at 15 m·s⁻¹. Gravity slows it by 9.8 m·s⁻¹ every second, so it stops after <strong>1.53 s</strong> at a height of <strong>11.5 m</strong>, then falls back. This is vertical projectile motion — the same maths predicts how long a stone takes to fall down a well or a ball to come down for a catch."},
    intro:"A <strong>projectile</strong> moves under gravity alone (air resistance ignored). Its acceleration is always g = 9.8 m·s⁻² <strong>downwards</strong> — on the way up, at the top and on the way down. At the highest point the velocity is zero, but the acceleration is still g. Use the equations of motion: v_f = v_i + gΔt, Δy = v_iΔt + ½gΔt², v_f² = v_i² + 2gΔy.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"↕️ Choose a direction", body:"Pick up (or down) as positive and stick to it. If up is positive, g = −9.8 m·s⁻²."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔁 Symmetry", body:"Time up = time down (to the same height). The speed when it returns to the launch height equals the launch speed."},
      {t:"gold", title:"📈 Graphs", body:"Position-time: a parabola. Velocity-time: a straight line with gradient g (crossing zero at the top). Acceleration-time: a horizontal line at g."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Time to the top", body:"Thrown up at 15 m·s⁻¹ (up positive).", answer:"0 = 15 − 9.8Δt → Δt = <strong>1.53 s</strong>."},
      {title:"Maximum height", body:"Same throw.", answer:"0 = 15² − 2 × 9.8 × Δy → Δy = 225/19.6 ≈ <strong>11.5 m</strong>."},
      {title:"Stone dropped down a 45 m mine shaft", body:"Released from rest. How long to the bottom?", answer:"45 = ½ × 9.8 × Δt² → Δt = √(90/9.8) ≈ <strong>3.03 s</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"v_f = v_i + gΔt · Δy = v_iΔt + ½gΔt² · v_f² = v_i² + 2gΔy",
    mistakes:["Saying acceleration is zero at the highest point (velocity is zero; acceleration is still g).","Mixing sign conventions halfway through.","Using 10 m·s⁻² when the paper says 9.8."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Sports science, safety at heights (falling objects on construction sites), mining, and designing fountains and fireworks."},
    activity:"A ball is dropped from a 20 m balcony. How fast is it moving when it hits the ground?",
    activityAnswer:"v_f² = 0 + 2 × 9.8 × 20 = 392 → v_f ≈ 19.8 m·s⁻¹ downwards.",
    examTip:"Write your sign convention at the top of your answer (“Take upwards as positive”). Examiners accept either, but you must be consistent.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Projectile",d:"Object moving under gravity only"},{w:"Free fall",d:"Motion with only gravity acting"},{w:"Gravitational acceleration (g)",d:"9.8 m·s⁻² downwards near Earth's surface"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"At the top of its flight, a ball thrown upward has:",opts:["Zero acceleration","Zero velocity","Maximum speed","Upward acceleration"],a:1,et:"Velocity is momentarily zero; acceleration is still 9.8 m·s⁻² down."},
      {q:"A ball thrown up at 9.8 m·s⁻¹ reaches the top after:",opts:["0.5 s","1 s","2 s","9.8 s"],a:1,et:"0 = 9.8 − 9.8Δt → Δt = 1 s."},
      {q:"The velocity-time graph of a projectile is:",opts:["A parabola","A straight line","Horizontal","A hyperbola"],a:1,et:"Constant acceleration gives a straight-line v-t graph with gradient g."},
    ],
    conclusion:"One acceleration, three equations. Choose a direction, list what you know, and pick the equation that fits."},
};

// ─── Module 1: Waves & Light ────────────────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.science.m1 = {
  0: {emoji:"🌊", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Wavelength, frequency and speed",
    illusText:"Waves carry energy across an ocean — or across the country to your radio — without carrying the water or air with them.",
    hook:{title:"How does Highveld Stereo reach your radio?", body:"Tune to 94.7 MHz and you're receiving a radio wave that oscillates 94.7 million times a second. It travels at the speed of light, 3 × 10⁸ m·s⁻¹, so each wave is about <strong>3.2 m long</strong>. The same equation, v = fλ, describes surf at Jeffreys Bay, sound at a concert and the Wi-Fi in your school."},
    intro:"A <strong>wave</strong> is a disturbance that transfers energy through a medium (or through space for electromagnetic waves). Key properties: <strong>amplitude</strong> (maximum displacement from rest), <strong>wavelength λ</strong> (distance between two consecutive identical points, e.g. crest to crest), <strong>frequency f</strong> (waves per second, Hz), <strong>period T</strong> (time for one wave, T = 1/f) and <strong>wave speed</strong> v = fλ.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📏 Reading a wave", body:"Crest: highest point. Trough: lowest point. Amplitude is measured from the rest position to a crest — not from trough to crest."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⚙️ v = fλ", body:"In one medium, speed is fixed: increase f and λ gets shorter. Sound in air travels at about 340 m·s⁻¹."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔊 Energy and amplitude", body:"Bigger amplitude → more energy (louder sound, bigger ocean wave). Frequency decides pitch for sound and colour for light."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"The radio wave", body:"f = 94.7 MHz, v = 3 × 10⁸ m·s⁻¹. Find λ.", answer:"λ = v/f = 3 × 10⁸ / 94.7 × 10⁶ ≈ <strong>3.17 m</strong>."},
      {title:"Ocean swell", body:"Waves 2 m apart pass a pier at 0.5 waves per second.", answer:"v = fλ = 0.5 × 2 = <strong>1 m·s⁻¹</strong>. Period T = 1/0.5 = 2 s."},
      {title:"Period", body:"A wave has a period of 4 s. Frequency?", answer:"f = 1/T = <strong>0.25 Hz</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"v = fλ · T = 1/f",
    formulaNote:"Convert MHz to Hz (× 10⁶) and cm to m before substituting.",
    mistakes:["Measuring amplitude from trough to crest.","Forgetting unit prefixes (MHz, kHz, nm).","Thinking a wave carries the medium along with it."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Telecommunications, radio and TV broadcasting, medical ultrasound, music production and oceanography."},
    activity:"A sound wave in air (v = 340 m·s⁻¹) has a frequency of 680 Hz. Find its wavelength.",
    activityAnswer:"λ = v/f = 340/680 = 0.5 m.",
    examTip:"Define terms exactly as in the CAPS glossary — e.g. “wavelength is the distance between two successive points in phase”.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Amplitude",d:"Maximum displacement from the rest position"},{w:"Frequency",d:"Number of waves per second (Hz)"},{w:"Wavelength",d:"Distance between two successive points in phase"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"v = 340 m·s⁻¹ and λ = 2 m. f =",opts:["170 Hz","680 Hz","342 Hz","0.006 Hz"],a:0,et:"f = v/λ = 340/2 = 170 Hz."},
      {q:"Period of a 50 Hz wave:",opts:["50 s","0.02 s","5 s","0.5 s"],a:1,et:"T = 1/f = 1/50 = 0.02 s."},
      {q:"Louder sound means larger:",opts:["Frequency","Wavelength","Amplitude","Speed"],a:2,et:"Amplitude relates to energy, so a louder sound has a larger amplitude."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Every wave has an amplitude, wavelength, frequency and speed, linked by v = fλ. Master this equation and the rest of the waves section is easy."},

  1: {emoji:"🏟️", subtitle:"Grade 10 · Two ways a wave can move",
    illusText:"A Mexican wave at FNB Stadium and a sound wave from the vuvuzela travel in different ways.",
    hook:{title:"The Mexican wave and the vuvuzela", body:"In a Mexican wave, fans stand up and sit down — they move <strong>up and down</strong> while the wave travels <strong>sideways</strong> around the stadium. That's a transverse wave. The sound from a vuvuzela squeezes and stretches the air <strong>in the same direction</strong> the sound travels. That's a longitudinal wave. Earthquakes send both kinds — and that's how seismologists in Gauteng locate mine tremors."},
    intro:"In a <strong>transverse wave</strong>, particles of the medium move <strong>perpendicular</strong> to the direction the wave travels (water waves, waves on a rope, light). In a <strong>longitudinal wave</strong>, particles move <strong>parallel</strong> to the direction of travel, forming <strong>compressions</strong> (squashed together) and <strong>rarefactions</strong> (spread out) — sound is longitudinal.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"〰️ Transverse", body:"Has crests and troughs. Wavelength = crest to crest. Light and all electromagnetic waves are transverse."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔈 Longitudinal", body:"Has compressions and rarefactions. Wavelength = compression to compression. Sound needs a medium — it cannot travel through a vacuum."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🌍 Seismic waves", body:"P-waves (primary) are longitudinal and arrive first. S-waves (secondary) are transverse and slower. The time gap tells you how far away the earthquake was."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Slinky", body:"You push a slinky back and forth along its length. Which type of wave?", answer:"<strong>Longitudinal</strong> — the coils move parallel to the direction the wave travels."},
      {title:"Sound speed", body:"A compression is 0.68 m from the next compression; f = 500 Hz.", answer:"λ = 0.68 m, so v = fλ = 500 × 0.68 = <strong>340 m·s⁻¹</strong>."},
      {title:"Space", body:"Why can't astronauts hear an explosion in space?", answer:"Sound is a <strong>mechanical longitudinal wave</strong> and needs particles to travel — space is (nearly) a vacuum."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Saying sound can travel through a vacuum.","Calling compressions “crests”.","Thinking particles travel along with the wave — they only vibrate about a fixed point."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Seismology (Council for Geoscience monitors tremors in mining areas), medical ultrasound scans, sonar on ships and acoustic design of halls."},
    activity:"Classify: (a) ripples on a dam (b) an ultrasound scan (c) light from the sun.",
    activityAnswer:"(a) transverse (b) longitudinal (sound) (c) transverse (electromagnetic).",
    examTip:"When asked to “describe” a wave type, mention both the particle motion AND the direction of energy transfer.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Transverse wave",d:"Particles vibrate perpendicular to the direction of propagation"},{w:"Longitudinal wave",d:"Particles vibrate parallel to the direction of propagation"},{w:"Compression",d:"Region where particles are closest together"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Sound is a:",opts:["Transverse wave","Longitudinal wave","Electromagnetic wave","Standing wave"],a:1,et:"Sound is longitudinal — compressions and rarefactions in the medium."},
      {q:"Which travels through a vacuum?",opts:["Sound","Light","P-waves","Water waves"],a:1,et:"Light is electromagnetic and needs no medium."},
      {q:"In a transverse wave, particles move:",opts:["Parallel to the wave","Perpendicular to the wave","Along with the wave","In circles only"],a:1,et:"Perpendicular to the direction of propagation."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Transverse: across the direction of travel. Longitudinal: along it. Know which is which and why sound can't cross space."},

  2: {emoji:"🚑", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Why the siren changes pitch as it passes",
    illusText:"An ambulance siren sounds higher as it races towards you and lower as it speeds away — the source hasn't changed, your position has.",
    hook:{title:"The ambulance on the N2", body:"An ambulance siren sounds at 700 Hz. As the ambulance races towards you at 30 m·s⁻¹ you hear about <strong>768 Hz</strong>; once it passes, it drops to about <strong>643 Hz</strong>. Traffic police speed guns use the same Doppler effect with radio waves to measure how fast you're driving."},
    intro:"The <strong>Doppler effect</strong> is the change in observed frequency when the source and listener move relative to each other. Approaching → waves are squashed → higher frequency. Moving apart → waves are stretched → lower frequency. For sound: f_L = (v ± v_L)/(v ± v_s) × f_s, where v is the speed of sound.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧮 Signs in the formula", body:"Listener moving towards the source: + v_L on top. Source moving towards the listener: − v_s at the bottom. (Make the fraction bigger when they approach.)"},
      {t:"teal", title:"🌌 Red shift", body:"Light from galaxies moving away from us is shifted to lower frequencies (towards red). This evidence shows the universe is expanding."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🩺 Medical use", body:"Doppler ultrasound measures blood flow speed in arteries and the heartbeat of an unborn baby."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Approaching ambulance", body:"f_s = 700 Hz, v_s = 30 m·s⁻¹, v = 340 m·s⁻¹, listener still.", answer:"f_L = 340/(340 − 30) × 700 ≈ <strong>768 Hz</strong>."},
      {title:"Ambulance moving away", body:"Same values.", answer:"f_L = 340/(340 + 30) × 700 ≈ <strong>643 Hz</strong>."},
      {title:"What stays the same?", body:"Does the siren's actual frequency change?", answer:"<strong>No</strong> — the source frequency is constant; only the observed frequency changes."},
    ],
    formula:"f_L = (v ± v_L)/(v ± v_s) · f_s",
    mistakes:["Swapping the signs (approaching must give a higher frequency — check your answer).","Using the speed of light for a sound question.","Saying the loudness change is the Doppler effect — it's the pitch (frequency)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Speed traps, weather radar (tracking storms over Gauteng), medical ultrasound and astronomy at the SKA radio telescope in the Karoo."},
    activity:"A car hooter (400 Hz) approaches a stationary listener at 20 m·s⁻¹. What frequency is heard? (v = 340 m·s⁻¹)",
    activityAnswer:"f_L = 340/(340 − 20) × 400 = 425 Hz.",
    examTip:"Learn the definition word for word: “the change in frequency (or pitch) of the sound detected by a listener because the sound source and the listener have different velocities relative to the medium.”",
    keyterms:[{w:"Doppler effect",d:"Change in observed frequency due to relative motion"},{w:"Red shift",d:"Light from a receding source shifts to lower frequency"},{w:"Source frequency",d:"Actual frequency emitted"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"As a source approaches, the observed frequency:",opts:["Decreases","Increases","Stays the same","Becomes zero"],a:1,et:"Waves are compressed ahead of the source, so the frequency increases."},
      {q:"Red shift shows that galaxies are:",opts:["Moving towards us","Moving away","Not moving","Getting hotter"],a:1,et:"Lower observed frequency means the source is receding."},
      {q:"Speed traps use the Doppler effect with:",opts:["Sound","Radio/microwaves","X-rays","Heat"],a:1,et:"Radar guns send radio/microwaves and measure the frequency change of the reflection."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Moving towards: higher frequency. Moving away: lower. The Doppler effect turns a pitch change into a speed measurement."},

  3: {emoji:"📡", subtitle:"Grade 10 · From radio waves to gamma rays",
    illusText:"Your phone, your microwave oven, the sun's UV and a hospital X-ray are all the same kind of wave — just different frequencies.",
    hook:{title:"Why Durban sun burns but your phone doesn't", body:"Your phone's 5G signal and the sun's ultraviolet light are both electromagnetic waves. The difference is frequency: UV photons carry far more energy each (E = hf), enough to damage skin cells. That's why the sun at a Durban beach can burn you in 20 minutes, while radio and phone waves don't break chemical bonds."},
    intro:"<strong>Electromagnetic (EM) waves</strong> are transverse waves of changing electric and magnetic fields. They need no medium and all travel at c = 3 × 10⁸ m·s⁻¹ in a vacuum. In order of <strong>increasing frequency</strong> (decreasing wavelength): radio, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays. Each <strong>photon</strong> carries energy E = hf (h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s).",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🌈 The spectrum and its uses", body:"Radio: broadcasting. Microwaves: cooking, cellphones, Wi-Fi. Infrared: TV remotes, night vision. Visible: seeing. UV: sterilising, sunburn. X-rays: medical imaging. Gamma: cancer treatment."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⚡ E = hf", body:"Higher frequency → more energy per photon → more penetrating and more dangerous. UV, X-rays and gamma rays are ionising — they can damage DNA."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧪 Penetrating ability", body:"Gamma rays pass through thick materials; radio waves are blocked by little more than hills and buildings at some frequencies. Lead aprons protect against X-rays."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Energy of green light", body:"f = 5.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz. Find the energy of one photon.", answer:"E = hf = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 5.5 × 10¹⁴ ≈ <strong>3.65 × 10⁻¹⁹ J</strong>."},
      {title:"Order by energy", body:"Arrange: X-rays, radio, visible.", answer:"<strong>Radio < visible < X-rays</strong> (energy increases with frequency)."},
      {title:"Wavelength from frequency", body:"A microwave oven uses 2.45 GHz. Find λ.", answer:"λ = c/f = 3 × 10⁸ / 2.45 × 10⁹ ≈ <strong>0.122 m (12.2 cm)</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"c = fλ · E = hf = hc/λ",
    mistakes:["Thinking higher wavelength means higher energy (it's the opposite).","Saying EM waves need a medium.","Forgetting to convert GHz/nm before substituting."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Radiography at public hospitals, cancer radiotherapy, telecoms (Vodacom, MTN), solar energy and astronomy."},
    activity:"Why is a lead apron used during an X-ray, but not when you use a cellphone?",
    activityAnswer:"X-ray photons have high energy (high frequency) and are ionising; lead absorbs them. Cellphone microwaves have low photon energy and are non-ionising.",
    examTip:"Remember the order with a mnemonic: “Raging Martians Invaded Venus Using X-ray Guns” (radio → gamma).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Photon",d:"A packet (quantum) of electromagnetic energy"},{w:"Ionising radiation",d:"Radiation energetic enough to remove electrons from atoms"},{w:"Speed of light (c)",d:"3 × 10⁸ m·s⁻¹ in a vacuum"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Which has the highest frequency?",opts:["Radio","Infrared","Ultraviolet","Gamma"],a:3,et:"Gamma rays have the highest frequency and energy."},
      {q:"All EM waves in a vacuum have the same:",opts:["Frequency","Wavelength","Speed","Energy"],a:2,et:"They all travel at 3 × 10⁸ m·s⁻¹."},
      {q:"Photon energy is proportional to:",opts:["Wavelength","Frequency","Amplitude","Speed"],a:1,et:"E = hf."},
    ],
    conclusion:"One family of waves, ordered by frequency. The higher the frequency, the more energy each photon carries — and the more careful you need to be."},

  4: {emoji:"🐟", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Light bending between materials",
    illusText:"A spearfisher must aim below where the fish appears to be — light bends when it leaves the water.",
    hook:{title:"Why the fish isn't where it looks", body:"A spearfisher at Sodwana Bay sees a fish, but if they aim straight at it they miss. Light from the fish bends as it leaves the water, so the fish looks <strong>higher and closer</strong> than it really is. The same bending traps light inside the glass fibres that bring fibre internet to your home."},
    intro:"<strong>Refraction</strong> is the change in direction of light when it passes from one medium to another, because its speed changes. The <strong>refractive index</strong> n = c/v. <strong>Snell's law</strong>: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ (angles measured from the normal). Going into a denser medium (higher n), light bends <strong>towards</strong> the normal; going out, it bends <strong>away</strong>.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📐 Angles from the normal", body:"The normal is a line perpendicular to the surface. Always measure angles of incidence and refraction from the normal, not from the surface."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔁 Critical angle", body:"Light going from a denser to a less dense medium: at the critical angle the refracted ray grazes the surface (90°). sin c = n₂/n₁. For water to air, c ≈ 48.8°."},
      {t:"gold", title:"💡 Total internal reflection", body:"If the angle of incidence is greater than the critical angle, all light reflects back inside. Optical fibres, endoscopes and diamonds' sparkle all use this."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Air into glass", body:"Light hits glass (n = 1.5) at 30° to the normal.", answer:"1 × sin 30° = 1.5 sin θ₂ → sin θ₂ = 0.333 → θ₂ ≈ <strong>19.5°</strong> (bent towards the normal)."},
      {title:"Critical angle of water", body:"n = 1.33.", answer:"sin c = 1/1.33 → c ≈ <strong>48.8°</strong>."},
      {title:"Speed in glass", body:"n = 1.5.", answer:"v = c/n = 3 × 10⁸/1.5 = <strong>2 × 10⁸ m·s⁻¹</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"n = c/v · n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ · sin c = n₂/n₁",
    mistakes:["Measuring angles from the surface instead of the normal.","Thinking total internal reflection can happen going into a denser medium.","Forgetting the calculator must be in degrees."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Fibre-optic broadband (Openserve, Vumatel), medical endoscopes, spectacles and camera lenses, and gemology (diamond cutting in Kimberley)."},
    activity:"Light travels from glass (n = 1.5) into air at 50°. What happens?",
    activityAnswer:"Critical angle: sin c = 1/1.5 → c ≈ 41.8°. 50° > 41.8°, so total internal reflection occurs — no light leaves the glass.",
    examTip:"Draw the normal as a dotted line in every ray diagram and label θ₁ and θ₂. The normal earns a mark on its own.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Refraction",d:"Bending of light due to a change in speed between media"},{w:"Refractive index",d:"n = c/v; no units"},{w:"Critical angle",d:"Angle of incidence giving a 90° refraction angle"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Light entering a denser medium bends:",opts:["Away from the normal","Towards the normal","Not at all","Back on itself"],a:1,et:"It slows down and bends towards the normal."},
      {q:"Total internal reflection needs light to travel:",opts:["Into a denser medium","Into a less dense medium above the critical angle","Along the normal","Through a vacuum"],a:1,et:"Denser to less dense, with incidence greater than the critical angle."},
      {q:"n of a material where light travels at 2.25 × 10⁸ m·s⁻¹:",opts:["1.33","0.75","1.5","2.25"],a:0,et:"n = c/v = 3/2.25 ≈ 1.33."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Light bends when its speed changes. Snell's law calculates it, and total internal reflection powers the internet."},

  5: {emoji:"🔍", subtitle:"Enrichment · How lenses and mirrors form images (beyond FET CAPS)",
    illusText:"Spectacles, phone cameras and car mirrors all bend or reflect light to make an image. Note: lens and mirror calculations are not examined in FET Physical Sciences.",
    hook:{title:"“Objects in mirror are closer than they appear”", body:"The passenger-side mirror on a car is <strong>convex</strong>. It shows a wider view, but makes cars look smaller — so further away. That's why it carries a warning. Your phone camera, a magnifying glass and the spectacles that help millions of South Africans read all work by forming images with lenses."},
    intro:"A <strong>converging (convex) lens</strong> bends parallel rays to meet at the <strong>focal point F</strong>; the distance to it is the <strong>focal length f</strong>. A <strong>diverging (concave) lens</strong> spreads rays out. The thin lens equation 1/f = 1/u + 1/v links object distance u and image distance v. <strong>Magnification</strong> m = v/u. Mirrors follow the same idea with reflection: concave mirrors converge, convex mirrors diverge.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔎 Real vs virtual images", body:"A real image forms where rays actually meet and can appear on a screen (a projector). A virtual image is where rays only appear to come from (a magnifying glass, a flat mirror)."},
      {t:"teal", title:"👓 Correcting eyesight", body:"Short-sighted eyes focus too early → concave lenses. Long-sighted eyes focus too late → convex lenses."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🪞 Mirrors", body:"Flat mirror: same size, upright, virtual, laterally inverted. Convex mirror: smaller, upright, wide view (security mirrors in shops). Concave: magnifies close up (make-up mirrors) or focuses light (solar cookers)."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Image distance", body:"Object 30 cm from a convex lens with f = 10 cm.", answer:"1/10 = 1/30 + 1/v → 1/v = 2/30 → v = <strong>15 cm</strong> (real image)."},
      {title:"Magnification", body:"Same lens.", answer:"m = v/u = 15/30 = <strong>0.5</strong> — the image is half the size (and inverted)."},
      {title:"Shop security mirror", body:"Why convex?", answer:"A convex mirror gives a <strong>wide field of view</strong>, so staff can see the whole aisle."},
    ],
    formula:"1/f = 1/u + 1/v · m = v/u",
    mistakes:["Mixing up converging (convex lens) and diverging (concave lens).","Saying a virtual image can be shown on a screen.","Confusing concave lenses with concave mirrors (they behave oppositely)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Optometry, photography and film, microscopes in hospital labs, solar cookers and telescopes like SALT in Sutherland."},
    activity:"A magnifying glass has f = 5 cm. Where must you hold an object to get a magnified, upright image?",
    activityAnswer:"Closer than the focal point — less than 5 cm from the lens.",
    examTip:"Not examined in NSC Physical Sciences, but Grade 8–9 Natural Sciences and optics courses at university build on these ideas.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Focal length",d:"Distance from the lens to the focal point"},{w:"Real image",d:"Formed where light rays actually meet"},{w:"Virtual image",d:"Rays only appear to come from it; can't be projected"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A shop security mirror is:",opts:["Flat","Concave","Convex","Transparent"],a:2,et:"Convex mirrors give a wide field of view."},
      {q:"Short-sightedness is corrected with:",opts:["Convex lenses","Concave lenses","Flat glass","Prisms"],a:1,et:"Concave (diverging) lenses move the focus back onto the retina."},
      {q:"An image you can project onto a screen is:",opts:["Virtual","Real","Upright always","Imaginary"],a:1,et:"Only real images can be formed on a screen."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Lenses and mirrors bend and bounce light to make images. Not examined in FET CAPS, but it's how cameras, glasses and telescopes work."},
};

// ─── Module 2: Electricity & Magnetism ──────────────────────────────────────
LESSON_DB.science.m2 = {
  0: {emoji:"⚡", subtitle:"Grade 11 · The force between charges",
    illusText:"Pull off a jersey in winter and hear it crackle — that's electrostatic force at work.",
    hook:{title:"Highveld lightning", body:"Gauteng has some of the highest lightning densities in the world. Inside storm clouds, collisions separate huge amounts of charge. When the electrostatic force becomes big enough, air breaks down and a lightning bolt carries the charge to the ground. Coulomb's law tells you how that force depends on the charges and the distance between them."},
    intro:"Like charges repel; unlike charges attract. <strong>Coulomb's law</strong>: the electrostatic force between two point charges is directly proportional to the product of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them: F = kQ₁Q₂/r², with k = 9 × 10⁹ N·m²·C⁻². Charge is quantised: Q = nqₑ, where qₑ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📉 Inverse-square law", body:"Double the distance → the force becomes ¼. Halve the distance → 4 times the force."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🧲 Electric field", body:"The region around a charge where another charge feels a force. E = F/q = kQ/r² (N·C⁻¹). Field lines point away from positive and towards negative charges."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔋 Charging", body:"Objects are charged by gaining or losing electrons (friction, contact, induction). Charge is conserved: it moves but isn't created or destroyed."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Force between two charges", body:"2 µC and 3 µC are 0.1 m apart.", answer:"F = 9 × 10⁹ × 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 3 × 10⁻⁶ / 0.1² = <strong>5.4 N</strong> (repulsive — both positive)."},
      {title:"Double the distance", body:"Move them to 0.2 m.", answer:"F becomes ¼ of 5.4 N = <strong>1.35 N</strong>."},
      {title:"Counting electrons", body:"A balloon gains a charge of −3.2 × 10⁻⁹ C. How many extra electrons?", answer:"n = Q/qₑ = 3.2 × 10⁻⁹ / 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = <strong>2 × 10¹⁰ electrons</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"F = kQ₁Q₂/r² · E = kQ/r² · Q = nqₑ",
    formulaNote:"Convert µC (× 10⁻⁶) and cm (÷ 100) first. Don't substitute signs into Coulomb's law — decide attract/repel separately.",
    mistakes:["Forgetting to square r.","Substituting negative charges and getting a “negative force”.","Converting µC as 10⁻³ instead of 10⁻⁶."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Lightning protection on buildings, photocopiers and laser printers, spray painting cars, and electronics manufacturing."},
    activity:"Two charges attract with 8 N. What is the force if both charges are doubled and the distance stays the same?",
    activityAnswer:"F ∝ Q₁Q₂, so doubling both multiplies F by 4 → 32 N.",
    examTip:"State Coulomb's law in words — it's a common 2-mark question. Include “point charges”, “product of charges” and “square of the distance”.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Coulomb (C)",d:"SI unit of charge"},{w:"Electric field",d:"Region where a charge experiences a force"},{w:"Quantisation of charge",d:"Charge comes in whole-number multiples of qₑ"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"If the distance between two charges is tripled, the force becomes:",opts:["3 times bigger","⅓","⅑","9 times bigger"],a:2,et:"Inverse-square: 1/3² = 1/9."},
      {q:"Two negative charges:",opts:["Attract","Repel","Don't interact","Cancel"],a:1,et:"Like charges repel."},
      {q:"An object loses electrons. It becomes:",opts:["Negative","Positive","Neutral","Magnetic"],a:1,et:"Losing negative electrons leaves a net positive charge."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Charges push and pull with a force that falls off with the square of the distance. Convert units carefully and the maths is straightforward."},

  1: {emoji:"🎄", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · How components share current and voltage",
    illusText:"When one old-style Christmas light blew, the whole string went dark. Your house is wired differently — and that's on purpose.",
    hook:{title:"Why your house isn't wired like old Christmas lights", body:"Old Christmas lights were wired in <strong>series</strong>: one path, so one broken bulb broke the whole circuit. Your home is wired in <strong>parallel</strong>: every plug and light gets the full 230 V and its own path, so switching off the kettle doesn't switch off the fridge. Understanding the difference is the first thing an electrician learns."},
    intro:"In a <strong>series</strong> circuit there is one path: the current is the same everywhere, voltages add up (V = V₁ + V₂ + …) and resistances add: R_s = R₁ + R₂ + … In a <strong>parallel</strong> circuit there are several paths: the voltage across each branch is the same, currents add (I = I₁ + I₂ + …) and 1/R_p = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + …",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"➡️ Series", body:"Adding a resistor in series increases total resistance, so the current drops. Bulbs in series get dimmer as you add more."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔀 Parallel", body:"Adding a branch in parallel decreases total resistance, so the total current from the source increases. The total parallel resistance is always smaller than the smallest branch."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔌 Measuring", body:"Ammeters go in series (they measure current through). Voltmeters go in parallel (they measure potential difference across)."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Series", body:"4 Ω and 6 Ω in series on a 12 V battery.", answer:"R = 10 Ω, I = V/R = 12/10 = <strong>1.2 A</strong>."},
      {title:"Parallel", body:"Same resistors in parallel.", answer:"1/R = 1/4 + 1/6 → R = <strong>2.4 Ω</strong>; I = 12/2.4 = <strong>5 A</strong> from the battery."},
      {title:"Branch currents", body:"Currents in each parallel branch.", answer:"I₄ = 12/4 = 3 A, I₆ = 12/6 = 2 A → total 5 A ✓"},
    ],
    formula:"Series: R = R₁ + R₂ · Parallel: 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ · V = IR",
    mistakes:["Forgetting to take the reciprocal at the end of a parallel calculation.","Putting a voltmeter in series.","Thinking current is “used up” along a series circuit."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Electrical wiring (a top artisan trade in SA), solar and inverter installations for load shedding, car electrics and electronics repair."},
    activity:"Three 6 Ω resistors are in parallel. What's the total resistance?",
    activityAnswer:"1/R = 3/6 → R = 2 Ω.",
    examTip:"Redraw complicated circuits so series and parallel parts are obvious, then simplify step by step from the inside out.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Series",d:"Components connected one after another — one path"},{w:"Parallel",d:"Components across the same two points — several paths"},{w:"Potential difference",d:"Energy per unit charge between two points (volts)"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"In a series circuit, the current is:",opts:["Different in each resistor","The same everywhere","Zero","Highest at the battery"],a:1,et:"There's only one path, so the current is the same throughout."},
      {q:"Two 10 Ω resistors in parallel give:",opts:["20 Ω","10 Ω","5 Ω","0.2 Ω"],a:2,et:"1/R = 1/10 + 1/10 = 1/5 → 5 Ω."},
      {q:"A voltmeter is connected:",opts:["In series","In parallel","Anywhere","Only at the battery"],a:1,et:"It measures the potential difference across a component."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Series shares the voltage; parallel shares the current. That's why homes are wired in parallel."},

  2: {emoji:"🔋", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Voltage, current and resistance",
    illusText:"Your phone charger, a car battery and a geyser element all obey one simple rule: V = IR.",
    hook:{title:"Why the car won't start", body:"A 12 V car battery has a small <strong>internal resistance</strong>. When you crank the starter, a huge current flows, and some voltage is “lost” inside the battery itself. An old battery has a bigger internal resistance, so the starter gets too little voltage and the car just clicks — a problem every mechanic in Mzansi diagnoses with Ohm's law."},
    intro:"<strong>Ohm's law</strong>: the current through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across it, at constant temperature: V = IR. Resistance R is measured in ohms (Ω). A battery's <strong>emf</strong> (ε) is the total energy it supplies per coulomb; because of <strong>internal resistance r</strong>, ε = I(R + r) and the terminal voltage V = ε − Ir.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📈 Ohmic conductors", body:"A graph of V against I is a straight line through the origin; the gradient is R. A filament bulb is non-ohmic: its resistance rises as it heats up."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🧮 Using V = IR", body:"Find any one of V, I, R from the other two. Apply it to the whole circuit or to a single component — but use matching values."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔋 Internal resistance", body:"More current → more “lost volts” (Ir) → lower terminal voltage. That's why headlights dim when the starter motor runs."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Simple circuit", body:"A 12 V battery is connected to a 4 Ω resistor (ignore internal resistance).", answer:"I = V/R = 12/4 = <strong>3 A</strong>."},
      {title:"Internal resistance", body:"ε = 12 V, r = 0.5 Ω, external R = 5.5 Ω.", answer:"I = ε/(R + r) = 12/6 = <strong>2 A</strong>. Terminal V = IR = 2 × 5.5 = <strong>11 V</strong>; lost volts = 1 V."},
      {title:"Charge flow", body:"How many electrons pass through a wire carrying 2 A for 10 s?", answer:"Q = It = 20 C → n = 20/1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = <strong>1.25 × 10²⁰ electrons</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"V = IR · ε = I(R + r) · Q = IΔt",
    mistakes:["Using ε instead of terminal voltage across the external resistor.","Forgetting that Ohm's law needs constant temperature.","Mixing values from different parts of the circuit."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Auto electricians, solar installers, electronics design, and checking whether an extension cord can handle a heater."},
    activity:"A phone charger supplies 5 V and 2 A. What is the effective resistance of the phone while charging?",
    activityAnswer:"R = V/I = 5/2 = 2.5 Ω.",
    examTip:"When a question mentions “emf” and “internal resistance”, use ε = I(R + r). It appears every year in Paper 1.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Ohm's law",d:"V ∝ I at constant temperature"},{w:"emf",d:"Maximum energy per coulomb a source can supply"},{w:"Internal resistance",d:"Resistance inside a battery"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"V = 9 V, R = 3 Ω. I =",opts:["27 A","3 A","0.33 A","12 A"],a:1,et:"I = V/R = 9/3 = 3 A."},
      {q:"Headlights dim when the starter runs because:",opts:["The emf drops","More lost volts across internal resistance","The bulbs cool","The resistance of the bulbs drops"],a:1,et:"A large current causes a large Ir drop inside the battery."},
      {q:"The gradient of a V-I graph for an ohmic conductor is:",opts:["Current","Resistance","Power","Charge"],a:1,et:"V = IR, so the gradient (V/I) is R."},
    ],
    conclusion:"V = IR and ε = I(R + r) explain most circuit questions — and most flat car batteries."},

  3: {emoji:"💡", subtitle:"Grade 10–12 · Paying for electricity",
    illusText:"Every rand on your prepaid electricity meter buys kilowatt-hours. Knowing how to calculate them saves your family money.",
    hook:{title:"What does the geyser really cost?", body:"A 3 kW geyser that heats for 2 hours a day uses 6 kWh a day — <strong>180 kWh a month</strong>. At about R3 per kWh, that's roughly <strong>R540 a month</strong>, often the biggest item on a household's electricity bill. A geyser timer or solar geyser can cut that sharply. The same maths decides how big an inverter you need for load shedding."},
    intro:"<strong>Electrical power</strong> is the rate at which electrical energy is converted: P = VI = I²R = V²/R (watts). <strong>Energy</strong> used = power × time: W = Pt. Electricity companies sell energy in <strong>kilowatt-hours (kWh)</strong>: 1 kWh = 1 000 W for 1 hour = 3.6 × 10⁶ J. Cost = energy (kWh) × price per kWh.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔌 Power ratings", body:"Appliances are labelled with their power: kettle ~2 000 W, fridge ~150 W, LED bulb ~5–10 W, TV ~100 W, geyser ~3 000 W."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🧮 kWh calculations", body:"Convert watts to kilowatts (÷ 1 000) and minutes to hours (÷ 60) before multiplying."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔋 Load shedding", body:"An inverter battery stores energy in kWh. A 1 kWh battery can run a 100 W load for about 10 hours (ignoring losses)."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Boiling the kettle", body:"A 2 000 W kettle runs for 3 minutes. Cost at R3/kWh?", answer:"E = 2 kW × 0.05 h = 0.1 kWh → <strong>30 cents</strong>."},
      {title:"The geyser", body:"3 kW for 2 h a day for 30 days at R3/kWh.", answer:"3 × 2 × 30 = 180 kWh → <strong>R540</strong> a month."},
      {title:"Current drawn", body:"The kettle runs on 230 V. Current?", answer:"I = P/V = 2 000/230 ≈ <strong>8.7 A</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"P = VI = I²R = V²/R · E = Pt · 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J",
    formulaNote:"Price per kWh varies by municipality and Eskom tariff — check your own slip.",
    mistakes:["Multiplying watts by hours and calling it kWh without dividing by 1 000.","Using minutes instead of hours.","Confusing power (W) with energy (kWh)."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Household budgeting, choosing appliances by energy rating, sizing solar panels and inverters, and running a small business with electricity costs."},
    activity:"Five 10 W LED bulbs run for 6 hours a day for 30 days. How many kWh is that, and what does it cost at R3/kWh?",
    activityAnswer:"5 × 10 W = 50 W = 0.05 kW. 0.05 × 6 × 30 = 9 kWh → R27.",
    examTip:"Show the unit conversions (W → kW, min → h) as separate steps. They are where most marks are lost.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Power",d:"Rate of energy conversion, in watts"},{w:"Kilowatt-hour",d:"Energy used by 1 kW for 1 hour"},{w:"Tariff",d:"Price charged per kWh"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A 1 000 W heater runs for 2 hours. Energy used:",opts:["2 kWh","2 000 kWh","500 kWh","0.5 kWh"],a:0,et:"1 kW × 2 h = 2 kWh."},
      {q:"P = VI. A device on 230 V draws 2 A. Power:",opts:["115 W","460 W","232 W","228 W"],a:1,et:"230 × 2 = 460 W."},
      {q:"Which uses the most energy in a month (same hours)?",opts:["LED bulb","TV","Fridge","Geyser"],a:3,et:"The geyser has by far the highest power rating."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Power × time = energy, and energy × tariff = rands. This is physics that pays your electricity bill."},

  4: {emoji:"🧭", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · Fields around magnets and currents",
    illusText:"A compass needle points north because the Earth is a giant magnet — and every current-carrying wire makes its own magnetic field.",
    hook:{title:"The scrapyard crane", body:"At a scrapyard in Germiston, a crane lifts whole car bodies with a giant <strong>electromagnet</strong>. Switch the current on and it becomes a powerful magnet; switch it off and the car drops. Hermanus is home to SANSA Space Science, which monitors the Earth's magnetic field to protect satellites and power grids from solar storms."},
    intro:"A <strong>magnetic field</strong> is the region where a magnet or moving charge feels a magnetic force. Field lines run from <strong>north to south</strong> outside a magnet; closer lines mean a stronger field. A <strong>current-carrying conductor</strong> creates a magnetic field around it. Use the <strong>right-hand rule</strong>: thumb in the direction of conventional current, fingers curl in the direction of the field.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🧲 Field patterns", body:"Bar magnet: lines leave N and enter S. Like poles repel, unlike poles attract. The Earth's geographic North Pole is actually near a magnetic south pole."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔄 Solenoids", body:"A coil of wire (solenoid) carrying current makes a field like a bar magnet. Adding an iron core and more turns, or more current, makes a stronger electromagnet."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🌍 Earth's magnetic field", body:"Protects us from charged particles from the sun. Auroras form where these particles hit the atmosphere near the poles."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Right-hand rule", body:"Current flows upward in a vertical wire. Which way does the field circle when viewed from above?", answer:"Thumb up, fingers curl <strong>anticlockwise</strong> (seen from above)."},
      {title:"Stronger electromagnet", body:"Give three ways to strengthen it.", answer:"<strong>More turns, more current, add a soft-iron core.</strong>"},
      {title:"Compass near a wire", body:"Why does a compass needle move when current flows in a nearby wire?", answer:"The current creates a <strong>magnetic field</strong> that exerts a force on the needle."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Drawing field lines that cross (they never do).","Using electron flow instead of conventional current with the right-hand rule.","Drawing lines from S to N outside the magnet."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Electric motors in everything from fans to taxis, MRI scanners, speakers, maglev trains and space-weather monitoring at SANSA."},
    activity:"Sketch the field between the N poles of two bar magnets facing each other. Where is the neutral point?",
    activityAnswer:"Lines curve away from both N poles; there's a neutral point (no field) midway between them.",
    examTip:"In field sketches, show arrows on the lines, keep lines from crossing, and make them denser near the poles.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Magnetic field",d:"Region where a magnetic force is felt"},{w:"Electromagnet",d:"Temporary magnet made by current in a coil"},{w:"Right-hand rule",d:"Gives the direction of the field around a current"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Outside a bar magnet, field lines go from:",opts:["S to N","N to S","Centre outwards","They don't have direction"],a:1,et:"By convention, from north to south outside the magnet."},
      {q:"Which does NOT strengthen an electromagnet?",opts:["More turns","More current","Iron core","Plastic core"],a:3,et:"Plastic is not magnetic; an iron core strengthens it."},
      {q:"Magnetic field lines:",opts:["Can cross","Never cross","Only exist inside magnets","Are always straight"],a:1,et:"Field lines never cross."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Moving charges make magnetic fields. That simple fact powers every motor, speaker and electromagnet."},

  5: {emoji:"🌬️", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Making electricity from magnetism",
    illusText:"Every power station — coal, nuclear, wind or hydro — spins a magnet inside coils of wire. That's Faraday's law.",
    hook:{title:"How a wind farm makes electricity", body:"Wind turbines near Jeffreys Bay turn a magnet inside coils of copper. As the magnetic flux through the coils changes, an <strong>emf is induced</strong> and current flows into the grid. Medupi's coal-fired turbines and Koeberg's nuclear turbines do the same thing — they only differ in what spins the generator."},
    intro:"<strong>Magnetic flux</strong> Φ = BA cos θ (weber, Wb) measures how much magnetic field passes through an area. <strong>Faraday's law</strong>: the emf induced in a coil is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux through it: ε = −NΔΦ/Δt. The minus sign is <strong>Lenz's law</strong>: the induced current opposes the change that caused it.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔄 Changing flux", body:"An emf is induced only while flux changes: move the magnet, move the coil, rotate it, or change the field strength. A stationary magnet in a coil induces nothing."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⬆️ More emf", body:"More turns (N), a stronger magnet (B), a bigger area (A) or faster motion (smaller Δt) all increase the induced emf."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🏭 Generators and transformers", body:"AC generators rotate coils in a field. Transformers use changing flux in an iron core to step voltage up for long-distance transmission and down for homes."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Induced emf", body:"A 200-turn coil's flux changes by 0.03 Wb in 0.1 s.", answer:"ε = NΔΦ/Δt = 200 × 0.03/0.1 = <strong>60 V</strong>."},
      {title:"Flux", body:"B = 0.2 T through an area of 0.05 m² at 90° to the coil's plane (θ = 0° to the normal).", answer:"Φ = BA cos 0° = <strong>0.01 Wb</strong>."},
      {title:"Lenz's law", body:"A magnet's N pole is pushed into a coil. What pole forms at that end of the coil?", answer:"A <strong>N pole</strong> — it opposes the approaching magnet."},
    ],
    formula:"Φ = BA cos θ · ε = −NΔΦ/Δt",
    mistakes:["Saying a stationary magnet in a coil induces current.","Using θ as the angle with the plane instead of with the normal.","Forgetting N, the number of turns."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Power generation (Eskom and independent producers), transformers on every street, induction stoves, wireless phone charging and electric guitar pickups."},
    activity:"How could you double the emf induced in a coil without changing the magnet?",
    activityAnswer:"Double the number of turns, or move the magnet twice as fast (halve Δt).",
    examTip:"State Faraday's law in words: “The emf induced is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux (flux linkage).”",
    keyterms:[{w:"Magnetic flux",d:"Φ = BA cos θ; measured in webers"},{w:"Induced emf",d:"Voltage created by changing flux"},{w:"Lenz's law",d:"Induced current opposes the change that caused it"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"An emf is induced in a coil when:",opts:["A magnet rests inside it","The magnetic flux through it changes","It is made of copper","It carries DC"],a:1,et:"Only a changing flux induces an emf."},
      {q:"ε for N = 100, ΔΦ = 0.02 Wb, Δt = 0.5 s:",opts:["4 V","1 V","10 V","0.04 V"],a:0,et:"100 × 0.02/0.5 = 4 V."},
      {q:"Power stations produce electricity using:",opts:["Static charge","Electromagnetic induction","Batteries","Solar cells only"],a:1,et:"Generators rely on electromagnetic induction."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Change the flux, induce an emf. Every turbine in South Africa runs on Faraday's law."},
};

