LESSON_DB.science.m3 = {
  0: {emoji:"⚛️", subtitle:"Grade 10 · What everything is made of",
    illusText:"Everything you touch — your phone, your lunch, your body — is made of about 90 kinds of atoms arranged in different ways.",
    hook:{title:"How old is that bone?", body:"Archaeologists working at sites like Mapungubwe use <strong>carbon dating</strong>. Living things contain a tiny fraction of carbon-14, an unstable isotope. After death it decays at a steady rate, so measuring what's left tells you the age. Isotopes are atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons — and they're the key to dating history."},
    intro:"An <strong>atom</strong> has a tiny nucleus of <strong>protons</strong> (positive) and <strong>neutrons</strong> (neutral), surrounded by <strong>electrons</strong> (negative). The <strong>atomic number Z</strong> = number of protons (decides the element). The <strong>mass number A</strong> = protons + neutrons. <strong>Isotopes</strong> have the same Z but different numbers of neutrons. Electrons occupy energy levels and orbitals: e.g. sodium is 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔢 Reading the symbol", body:"²³₁₁Na: Z = 11 protons, A = 23, so 12 neutrons. A neutral atom has 11 electrons."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⚖️ Relative atomic mass", body:"The average mass of an element's isotopes, weighted by abundance. Chlorine: 75% Cl-35 and 25% Cl-37 → 35.5."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔋 Electron configuration", body:"Fill orbitals in order of increasing energy: 1s, 2s, 2p, 3s, 3p, 4s, 3d … Outer (valence) electrons decide how an element reacts."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Particles in an atom", body:"³⁵₁₇Cl.", answer:"17 protons, <strong>18 neutrons</strong>, 17 electrons (neutral atom)."},
      {title:"Relative atomic mass", body:"Chlorine: 75% Cl-35, 25% Cl-37.", answer:"0.75 × 35 + 0.25 × 37 = <strong>35.5</strong>."},
      {title:"Configuration of O", body:"Z = 8.", answer:"<strong>1s² 2s² 2p⁴</strong> — 6 valence electrons."},
    ],
    formula:"neutrons = A − Z",
    mistakes:["Saying isotopes have different numbers of protons.","Mixing up mass number and atomic number.","Forgetting that ions have more or fewer electrons than protons."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Archaeology and carbon dating, nuclear medicine (isotopes for cancer scans at hospitals), Koeberg nuclear power, and chemistry in every industry."},
    activity:"How many protons, neutrons and electrons are in the ion ²⁴₁₂Mg²⁺?",
    activityAnswer:"12 protons, 12 neutrons, 10 electrons (it has lost 2).",
    examTip:"For ions, adjust only the electrons: a + charge means electrons were lost; a − charge means electrons were gained.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Atomic number",d:"Number of protons in the nucleus"},{w:"Isotopes",d:"Atoms of the same element with different numbers of neutrons"},{w:"Valence electrons",d:"Electrons in the outermost energy level"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Neutrons in ²⁷₁₃Al:",opts:["13","14","27","40"],a:1,et:"27 − 13 = 14."},
      {q:"Isotopes of an element differ in their number of:",opts:["Protons","Electrons","Neutrons","Charge"],a:2,et:"Same protons, different neutrons."},
      {q:"Electron configuration of Na (Z = 11) ends in:",opts:["2p⁶","3s¹","3s²","3p¹"],a:1,et:"1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Protons decide the element, neutrons make isotopes and electrons decide the chemistry. Every topic in chemistry builds on this."},

  1: {emoji:"🔗", subtitle:"Grade 10–11 · How atoms hold together",
    illusText:"Salt, water and copper wire are held together in three completely different ways.",
    hook:{title:"Salt, water and copper", body:"Salt from the pans near Gqeberha is held together by <strong>ionic</strong> bonds — it's hard and brittle and conducts electricity when dissolved. Water molecules share electrons in <strong>covalent</strong> bonds. The copper mined at Phalaborwa has <strong>metallic</strong> bonds — a sea of electrons that lets it carry current through every electrical wire in your home."},
    intro:"Atoms bond to reach a more stable arrangement of electrons. <strong>Ionic bonding</strong>: electrons transfer from a metal to a non-metal, forming oppositely charged ions held by electrostatic attraction (NaCl). <strong>Covalent bonding</strong>: non-metal atoms share pairs of electrons (H₂O, CH₄, CO₂). <strong>Metallic bonding</strong>: positive metal ions in a sea of delocalised electrons.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"⚡ Electronegativity", body:"How strongly an atom attracts bonding electrons. Big difference (> 2.1) → ionic. Small difference → polar covalent. Zero → non-polar covalent."},
      {t:"teal", title:"✏️ Lewis diagrams", body:"Show valence electrons as dots. In H₂O, oxygen shares one pair with each hydrogen and keeps two lone pairs — giving water its bent shape."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧱 Properties follow bonding", body:"Ionic: high melting point, conduct when molten or dissolved. Covalent molecules: low melting points, don't conduct. Metals: conduct, malleable, ductile."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Classify the bond", body:"NaCl, H₂O, Cu.", answer:"NaCl: <strong>ionic</strong>. H₂O: <strong>polar covalent</strong>. Cu: <strong>metallic</strong>."},
      {title:"Ions formed", body:"Magnesium reacts with oxygen. Which ions form?", answer:"Mg loses 2 electrons → <strong>Mg²⁺</strong>; O gains 2 → <strong>O²⁻</strong>; formula MgO."},
      {title:"Why does copper conduct?", body:"Explain.", answer:"Its <strong>delocalised electrons</strong> are free to move through the metal lattice."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Saying ionic compounds conduct as solids.","Calling the attraction in NaCl “sharing” electrons.","Forgetting lone pairs in Lewis diagrams."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Materials science, mining and metallurgy (copper, platinum), pharmaceuticals and designing new materials for batteries and solar panels."},
    activity:"Draw the Lewis diagram of methane (CH₄). How many bonding pairs and lone pairs does C have?",
    activityAnswer:"Four single C–H bonds (4 bonding pairs), no lone pairs on carbon; tetrahedral shape.",
    examTip:"Link properties to bonding in your explanations: “NaCl has a high melting point because strong electrostatic forces between ions must be overcome.”",
    keyterms:[{w:"Ionic bond",d:"Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions"},{w:"Covalent bond",d:"Sharing of electron pairs between atoms"},{w:"Electronegativity",d:"Tendency of an atom to attract bonding electrons"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"The bond in NaCl is:",opts:["Covalent","Ionic","Metallic","Hydrogen"],a:1,et:"Electrons transfer from Na to Cl, forming ions."},
      {q:"Metals conduct because of:",opts:["Lone pairs","Delocalised electrons","Ions in solution","Protons"],a:1,et:"Free-moving electrons carry the current."},
      {q:"Water molecules are:",opts:["Non-polar","Polar","Ionic","Metallic"],a:1,et:"O is more electronegative than H and the molecule is bent, so it's polar."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Transfer, share or pool electrons — the type of bond explains a material's properties."},

  2: {emoji:"💧", subtitle:"Grade 11 · Forces between molecules",
    illusText:"Why does water boil at 100 °C but methane at −162 °C? The difference is in the forces between their molecules.",
    hook:{title:"Why ice floats in your cooldrink", body:"Almost every solid sinks in its own liquid — but ice floats. Strong <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong> lock water molecules into an open hexagonal pattern when it freezes, making ice less dense. That's why lakes freeze from the top down and fish survive winter, and why water boils at such a high temperature for such a small molecule."},
    intro:"<strong>Intermolecular forces (IMFs)</strong> act <strong>between</strong> molecules (not inside them). From weakest to strongest: <strong>London (dispersion) forces</strong> (all molecules, bigger molecules → stronger), <strong>dipole-dipole forces</strong> (between polar molecules) and <strong>hydrogen bonds</strong> (H bonded to N, O or F). Stronger IMFs mean higher boiling and melting points, lower vapour pressure and higher surface tension.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🌡️ Boiling points", body:"Boiling overcomes IMFs, not covalent bonds. Water (hydrogen bonds) boils at 100 °C; methane (London forces only) boils at −162 °C."},
      {t:"teal", title:"💨 Vapour pressure", body:"Weak IMFs → molecules escape easily → high vapour pressure → evaporates quickly (e.g. paraffin and nail-polish remover evaporate faster than water)."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🫧 Surface tension & capillarity", body:"Strong hydrogen bonds let insects walk on water and help water rise up plant stems."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Rank boiling points", body:"CH₄, HCl, H₂O.", answer:"<strong>CH₄ < HCl < H₂O</strong> (London < dipole-dipole < hydrogen bonding)."},
      {title:"Same IMF, different size", body:"Why does octane boil higher than methane?", answer:"Both have only London forces, but octane is <strong>much larger</strong>, so its London forces are stronger."},
      {title:"Hydrogen bonding?", body:"Which can hydrogen-bond: NH₃ or CH₄?", answer:"<strong>NH₃</strong> — H is bonded to N."},
    ],
    mistakes:["Saying boiling breaks covalent bonds inside molecules.","Thinking any molecule containing H can hydrogen-bond.","Confusing intermolecular with intramolecular forces."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Fuel design (petrol blends), pharmaceuticals, cosmetics, refrigeration, and understanding why water is so special for life."},
    activity:"Explain why ethanol (C₂H₅OH) evaporates more slowly than diethyl ether, even though they have similar sizes.",
    activityAnswer:"Ethanol has hydrogen bonds (O–H); ether has only dipole-dipole and London forces. Stronger IMFs in ethanol → lower vapour pressure → slower evaporation.",
    examTip:"In explanations always name the IMF, compare its strength, and link it to the energy needed to overcome it.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Intermolecular force",d:"Attractive force between molecules"},{w:"Hydrogen bond",d:"Strong dipole attraction involving H bonded to N, O or F"},{w:"Vapour pressure",d:"Pressure exerted by a vapour in equilibrium with its liquid"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Which has hydrogen bonding?",opts:["CH₄","H₂O","CO₂","Cl₂"],a:1,et:"H bonded to O."},
      {q:"Stronger IMFs mean:",opts:["Lower boiling point","Higher vapour pressure","Higher boiling point","Faster evaporation"],a:2,et:"More energy is needed to separate the molecules."},
      {q:"Boiling water breaks:",opts:["O–H covalent bonds","Hydrogen bonds between molecules","Protons","Ionic bonds"],a:1,et:"Only the intermolecular forces are overcome."},
    ],
    conclusion:"IMFs explain boiling points, evaporation and why ice floats. Identify the strongest IMF and you can predict the property."},

  3: {emoji:"🚗", subtitle:"Grade 11 · How gases behave",
    illusText:"Check your tyre pressure before a long trip on the N1 — and again after: hot tyres have higher pressure.",
    hook:{title:"Tyre pressure on a hot N1 trip", body:"A tyre pumped to 220 kPa at 20 °C heats up to 50 °C on a long drive from Joburg to Bloemfontein. The volume barely changes, so the pressure rises to about <strong>243 kPa</strong>. That's why tyre makers say to check pressure when tyres are cold — and why overloaded, overheated tyres can blow out."},
    intro:"An <strong>ideal gas</strong> obeys the gas laws exactly (real gases are close at normal conditions). <strong>Boyle</strong>: p₁V₁ = p₂V₂ (constant T). <strong>Charles</strong>: V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂ (constant p). <strong>Gay-Lussac</strong>: p₁/T₁ = p₂/T₂ (constant V). Combined: p₁V₁/T₁ = p₂V₂/T₂. <strong>Ideal gas equation</strong>: pV = nRT (R = 8.31 J·K⁻¹·mol⁻¹). Temperatures must be in <strong>kelvin</strong>: T(K) = t(°C) + 273.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🌡️ Always use kelvin", body:"0 K is absolute zero. Doubling 20 °C to 40 °C does NOT double the kinetic energy — use 293 K and 313 K."},
      {t:"teal", title:"📦 SI units in pV = nRT", body:"p in Pa, V in m³ (1 dm³ = 10⁻³ m³), T in K. At STP (273 K, 101.3 kPa), 1 mol of gas occupies 22.4 dm³."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔬 Kinetic theory", body:"Gas pressure comes from molecules hitting the walls. Hotter → faster molecules → harder, more frequent collisions → more pressure."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"The hot tyre", body:"220 kPa at 20 °C, heated to 50 °C, volume constant.", answer:"p₂ = 220 × 323/293 ≈ <strong>242.5 kPa</strong>."},
      {title:"Boyle's law", body:"A syringe holds 2 dm³ of air at 100 kPa. It is compressed to 0.5 dm³.", answer:"p₂ = 100 × 2/0.5 = <strong>400 kPa</strong>."},
      {title:"pV = nRT", body:"How many moles in 22.4 dm³ at 101.3 kPa and 273 K?", answer:"n = pV/RT = 101 300 × 0.0224/(8.31 × 273) ≈ <strong>1.00 mol</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"p₁V₁/T₁ = p₂V₂/T₂ · pV = nRT · T(K) = t(°C) + 273",
    mistakes:["Using °C instead of K.","Using dm³ and kPa in pV = nRT without converting.","Forgetting which variable is held constant."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Vehicle maintenance, scuba diving, weather forecasting, gas cylinders (LPG for cooking) and chemical engineering at Sasol."},
    activity:"A balloon has a volume of 3 dm³ at 27 °C. What's its volume at 87 °C (constant pressure)?",
    activityAnswer:"V₂ = 3 × 360/300 = 3.6 dm³.",
    examTip:"Write down the given values in a table (p₁, V₁, T₁, p₂, V₂, T₂) with the temperatures already in kelvin before you substitute.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Ideal gas",d:"Gas that obeys the gas laws at all temperatures and pressures"},{w:"Absolute zero",d:"0 K (−273 °C), the lowest possible temperature"},{w:"STP",d:"Standard temperature and pressure: 273 K and 101.3 kPa"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"25 °C in kelvin:",opts:["248 K","298 K","25 K","273 K"],a:1,et:"25 + 273 = 298 K."},
      {q:"At constant temperature, halving the volume of a gas makes its pressure:",opts:["Halve","Double","Stay the same","Quadruple"],a:1,et:"Boyle's law: p ∝ 1/V."},
      {q:"Gas pressure is caused by:",opts:["Molecules repelling","Molecules colliding with the walls","Gravity","Heat only"],a:1,et:"Collisions of molecules with the container walls."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Gases follow simple laws — as long as you use kelvin and consistent units."},

  4: {emoji:"🧪", subtitle:"Grade 11–12 · Acids, bases and the pH scale",
    illusText:"Vinegar on your slap chips, an antacid for heartburn and lime on acidic farm soil — all acid-base chemistry.",
    hook:{title:"Why farmers add lime to their soil", body:"Much of South Africa's farmland, especially in Mpumalanga and KZN, is <strong>acidic</strong>. Maize grows poorly below about pH 5.5. Farmers spread agricultural lime (a base) to neutralise the acid and raise the pH. Your stomach uses acid to digest food, and an antacid tablet does the same neutralisation when you have heartburn."},
    intro:"An <strong>acid</strong> donates protons (H⁺) and a <strong>base</strong> accepts protons (Brønsted-Lowry). <strong>Strong</strong> acids/bases ionise completely (HCl, NaOH); <strong>weak</strong> ones only partly (vinegar's ethanoic acid). <strong>pH</strong> = −log[H₃O⁺]: below 7 is acidic, 7 neutral, above 7 basic. In <strong>neutralisation</strong>, acid + base → salt + water. A <strong>titration</strong> uses this to find an unknown concentration.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"📏 The pH scale", body:"Each pH unit is a factor of 10. pH 3 is ten times more acidic than pH 4. Stomach acid ≈ pH 2; pure water = 7; bleach ≈ 12–13."},
      {t:"teal", title:"⚗️ Titration", body:"Add one solution from a burette until the indicator changes colour (endpoint). Then use c_aV_a/(c_bV_b) = n_a/n_b from the balanced equation."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🔄 Conjugate pairs", body:"HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻. HCl and Cl⁻ are a conjugate acid-base pair; so are H₃O⁺ and H₂O."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"pH of a strong acid", body:"0.01 mol·dm⁻³ HCl.", answer:"[H₃O⁺] = 0.01 → pH = −log(0.01) = <strong>2</strong>."},
      {title:"pH of a strong base", body:"0.01 mol·dm⁻³ NaOH.", answer:"[OH⁻] = 0.01 → pOH = 2 → pH = 14 − 2 = <strong>12</strong>."},
      {title:"Titration", body:"25 cm³ of 0.1 mol·dm⁻³ NaOH is neutralised by 12.5 cm³ HCl (1 : 1). Find c(HCl).", answer:"c_a = c_bV_b/V_a = 0.1 × 25/12.5 = <strong>0.2 mol·dm⁻³</strong>."},
    ],
    formula:"pH = −log[H₃O⁺] · pH + pOH = 14 · c_aV_a/(c_bV_b) = n_a/n_b",
    mistakes:["Confusing strong/weak (degree of ionisation) with concentrated/dilute (amount per volume).","Forgetting the mole ratio from the balanced equation in titrations.","Using natural log instead of log base 10."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Agriculture (soil testing), water treatment at municipal plants, pharmacy, food processing and swimming-pool chemistry."},
    activity:"What is the pH of 0.001 mol·dm⁻³ HNO₃ (a strong acid)?",
    activityAnswer:"[H₃O⁺] = 0.001 → pH = 3.",
    examTip:"In titration questions, write the balanced equation first — the mole ratio is often the step that decides the answer.",
    keyterms:[{w:"Brønsted-Lowry acid",d:"Proton (H⁺) donor"},{w:"Strong acid",d:"Ionises completely in water"},{w:"Neutralisation",d:"Acid + base → salt + water"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"A solution with pH 9 is:",opts:["Acidic","Neutral","Basic","Strongly acidic"],a:2,et:"Above 7 is basic."},
      {q:"pH of 0.1 mol·dm⁻³ HCl:",opts:["0.1","1","2","13"],a:1,et:"−log(0.1) = 1."},
      {q:"A weak acid:",opts:["Is always dilute","Ionises only partially","Has pH 7","Can't be neutralised"],a:1,et:"Weak refers to partial ionisation, not concentration."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Acids donate, bases accept, and pH tells you how much. Titrations turn that into precise concentrations."},

  5: {emoji:"🔋", subtitle:"Grade 12 · Chemistry that makes and uses electricity",
    illusText:"A car battery makes electricity from chemistry. An aluminium smelter does the reverse — it uses electricity to make metal.",
    hook:{title:"Batteries and the Richards Bay smelter", body:"Your phone battery and the car battery are <strong>galvanic cells</strong>: a chemical reaction pushes electrons through a circuit. At Hillside in Richards Bay, one of the world's largest aluminium smelters, huge currents drive the opposite process — <strong>electrolysis</strong> — to pull aluminium out of its ore. Same chemistry, opposite directions."},
    intro:"Electrochemistry is about <strong>redox</strong> reactions: <strong>oxidation</strong> is loss of electrons (at the <strong>anode</strong>), <strong>reduction</strong> is gain of electrons (at the <strong>cathode</strong>). A <strong>galvanic cell</strong> converts chemical energy to electrical energy (spontaneous). An <strong>electrolytic cell</strong> uses electrical energy to drive a non-spontaneous reaction. Standard cell emf: E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode.",
    concepts:[
      {t:"green", title:"🔋 Galvanic cell (Zn–Cu)", body:"Zn is oxidised (anode, −), Cu²⁺ is reduced (cathode, +). A salt bridge keeps the solutions neutral. E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = 1.10 V."},
      {t:"teal", title:"🔌 Electrolysis", body:"Used to electroplate jewellery, refine copper, and produce chlorine and sodium hydroxide (chlor-alkali industry). The anode is connected to the positive terminal."},
      {t:"gold", title:"🧠 Memory aids", body:"“OIL RIG” — Oxidation Is Loss, Reduction Is Gain. “AN OX, RED CAT” — oxidation at the anode, reduction at the cathode."},
    ],
    examples:[
      {title:"Cell emf", body:"Zn²⁺/Zn (−0.76 V) and Cu²⁺/Cu (+0.34 V) under standard conditions.", answer:"E°cell = 0.34 − (−0.76) = <strong>1.10 V</strong>; positive → spontaneous."},
      {title:"Half-reactions", body:"Write them for the Zn–Cu cell.", answer:"Anode: <strong>Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻</strong>. Cathode: <strong>Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu</strong>."},
      {title:"Electroplating", body:"To copper-plate a key, which electrode is the key?", answer:"The <strong>cathode</strong> — Cu²⁺ ions are reduced onto it."},
    ],
    formula:"E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode",
    mistakes:["Mixing up anode and cathode signs between galvanic and electrolytic cells.","Flipping the sign of E° when using the subtraction formula.","Writing electrons on the wrong side of a half-reaction."],
    callout:{title:"Where you'll use this", body:"Battery technology (EVs and home solar storage), mining and metal refining, corrosion protection on bridges and ships, and the chlor-alkali industry that makes water-treatment chemicals."},
    activity:"Using standard potentials, is the reaction Cu + Zn²⁺ → Cu²⁺ + Zn spontaneous?",
    activityAnswer:"Cathode would be Zn²⁺/Zn (−0.76), anode Cu²⁺/Cu (0.34): E°cell = −0.76 − 0.34 = −1.10 V. Negative → not spontaneous.",
    examTip:"Always state the standard conditions when asked: 1 mol·dm⁻³ solutions, 101.3 kPa and 298 K (25 °C).",
    keyterms:[{w:"Oxidation",d:"Loss of electrons"},{w:"Galvanic cell",d:"Converts chemical energy into electrical energy"},{w:"Electrolysis",d:"Using electricity to drive a non-spontaneous reaction"}],
    quiz:[
      {q:"Oxidation happens at the:",opts:["Cathode","Anode","Salt bridge","Voltmeter"],a:1,et:"AN OX — oxidation at the anode."},
      {q:"E°cell for a spontaneous reaction is:",opts:["Negative","Zero","Positive","Always 1.10 V"],a:2,et:"A positive emf means the reaction is spontaneous."},
      {q:"To electroplate an object, it must be the:",opts:["Anode","Cathode","Electrolyte","Salt bridge"],a:1,et:"Metal ions are reduced onto the cathode."},
    ],
    conclusion:"Electrons flow from oxidation to reduction. Harness them for batteries, or force them with electricity to make metals."},
};

